sequenceDiagram
  %% Container runtime (ADR 0250 step 8, docs/reference/development-containers.md#sleep-and-wake): freeze a development container with its memory in swap and leave its Route on the wake page. The hibernation sweep admits the same kind after an idle hour; the command admits it at once.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: instance:sleep
  %% kind: instance.sleep
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · sweep
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>Instance container
  CLI->>GW: instance:sleep INSTANCE
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit instance:sleep INSTANCE`
  %%> - syntax: `<instance-id> [--json]`
  %%> Callers:
  %%> - the command, at once
  %%> - the idle sleep of the runtime hibernator, every 10 min, after it read no HTTP, wake or T3 activity for an hour and no running turn (docs/reference/development-containers.md#sleep-and-wake)
  Note over GW: Last read of T3 threads<br/>Conn thread index
  %%> When:
  %%> - after the refusals and the replay of a repeated request, before the Instance lock; within 3 seconds
  %%> - only while Conn and its index switch `ORBIT_CONN_THREAD_INDEX_POLL` are on; the sleep switch does not matter
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - reads the container T3 server's threads once more, so the index keeps them while the Instance sleeps (docs/reference/conn.md#thread-index)
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - the index keeps its earlier rows; the sleep goes on
  Note over GW: Admit the sleep
  %%> When:
  %%> - an active development container that is not a default and not a nested-fleet Instance; take the Instance lock
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - no container, a default, a nested-fleet Instance, an active task or lifecycle work starts no run
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-sleep
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID
  SW->>N: Mark the Route asleep<br/>Ansible · tags asleep
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - checks that Caddy's loaded site of the Instance has the asleep rule, else `instance.sleep_route_unready`
  %%> - writes the asleep marker, so each request from now on gets the wake page
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the marker comes before the freeze: a request during the freeze gets the wake page, not a hang
  SW->>N: Freeze the container<br/>Ansible · tags freeze
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes `max` to the container cgroup's `memory.swap.max` (Incus writes 0), `incus pause`, then a reclaim of its memory
  %%> - the processes and their state stay; their memory goes to swap, about 13 MiB stays in RAM
  %%> - a frozen or stopped container stays as it is
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the reclaim follows the pause, so no running process pulls a page back
  %%> - swap opens for this cgroup only and only while it sleeps: a running container never swaps
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - a failed swap write or pause writes 0 back and removes the marker, `instance.container_freeze_failed`; the container serves as before
  opt more than 32 MiB of anonymous memory stays in RAM
    SW->>N: Stop the container<br/>Ansible · resume, incus stop
    %%> Why:
    %%> - swap is full; a frozen container would hold RAM, which a sleep exists to free
    %%> Result:
    %%> - `sleep: stopped`; the next wake boots it behind the same marker
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - the resumed container runs: swap closes again, the marker goes, `instance.container_freeze_failed`
  end
  alt the run passes
    %% path: main
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result
    Note over GW: Record asleep
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the runtime's `power_state` is `stopped` for a frozen and a stopped container; the result says `sleep: frozen` or `sleep: stopped`
    %%> - `t3_turn` is `idle` when the idle sleep started it, which read T3 first, and `not_checked` for the command
    %%> - the Conn layer shows its T3 server asleep; the hourly renewal skips it
  else a step fails
    %% path: failed
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code
    %%> Result:
    %%> - `power_state` stays as it was. A marker left on a running container is harmless: the next request wakes the Instance, which removes it
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - the same command again
  end
  loop until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
    GW-->>CLI: Status and result
  end
